A||||1||Darekin||Issues with my 85 GT 2.8L..need help.||||1||
Z||000000||Darekin||07-26-2001||07:27 PM||||My timing is jumping around alot. at idle it will be between 6 and 12, and then the next second be all the way at 30... And at cruise.. it will be at 30... and then jump all the way to 52... then suddenly it will be normal again... me and my mechanic are having severe troubles trying to pin this down.<P>List of replaced items:<BR>New Plugs, New wires,  New Cap, New Rotor, New Distributor assemply including shaft, New coil, New EGR Valve, New EGR Solenoid, New Ignition Control module. Replaced 1 Injector that was bad with a new one , the rest are fine.. had them tested.<P>We have been trying to figure this out for a month now, and can't seem to figure anything out. Also... My engine misfires.. not sure if its related... We have set the timing multiple times and it will work fine.. and then the next day not fine... so any help would be appreciated.||||reg||1
Z||000001||terryk||07-26-2001||09:48 PM||||If you ground ALDL A and B is the timing steady? This locks the timing at 10 degrees.<P>The timing is mainly controlled by the map sensor and ECM coolant sensor. The lookup table in the eprom is RPM vs MAP so if the map is jumping around (because of a bad map or vacuum leak, noisy/bad +5V reference or bad map ground), the timing will move around in the table and thus jump. If the ECM coolant sensor is noisy it will add and subtract advance (+10 to -4 degrees of advance).<P>Next, if the reference signal from the dist is jumping (noisy or bad ground), that will cause it too. <P>Since the reference pulses from the distributor trigger the injectors, you might scope the injectors and see if they are moving around in time. If they are steady and the timing is moving, them the ECM or map is suspect. If the injectors are jumping, then the module or pickup coil could be bad.<P>Could be a bad ECM also, but if shorting out ALDL A and B makes it stop moving, the ECM is ok.<P>The timing is also controlled by the EGR. If the ECM thinks the EGR should be active, it will add timing. it activates the EGR based on TPS and MAP. I doubt this one other than a leaking EGR will cause the MAP to change. <P>Ah, so the TPS. If it's jumping it can make the ECM think EGR should be active and it will add timing. I doubt this one too.<P>I'd check the MAP first. Disconnect the vacuum line and plug it. See if the timing is steady. If so, add some vacuum and the timing should move. The idle column in the code is 36 degrees so it should idle *around* there. Maybe a bit higher. <P>Second check the ECM coolant sensor. Watch the timing and tap on the sensor. See if the timing jumps.<P>Next check the +5V for noise. That requires a scope. <P>Next, check the grounds to the MAP and dist and chassis.<P>I'm betting it's map related.<P>A final note, jumping timing is not necessarily a sign something is wrong. Running bad is..... Later ECM's use the timing for idle smoothing so it jumps normally. I don't think the Fiero ECM does this, but unstable timing is not a sign something is wrong. <P>The spark table in the eprom is 36 degrees at 20 kpa, 40 at 25, 46 at 30....... If the map is between 25 and 30 kpa of vacuum, it can jump between those advance values. Make sure you're not fixing something that ain't broke.<P> <p>[This message has been edited by terryk (edited 07-26-2001).]||||reg||1
